</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To determine the change in equilibrium concentration of FeCN$_4-$, we first write the equilibrium equation for the formation of the complex ion:
\[
\text{Fe}^{2+} + 6\text{CN}_2^- \rightleftharpoons \text{FeCN}_6^{4-}
\]
Given:
\[
K_{eq} = 1.2 \times 10^{20}
\]
Initial concentration of Fe\(^{2+}\): 1.0 M
 Initial concentration of CN\(^-\): 0.25 moles in 1 L solution, so 0.25 M
 The reaction is:
\[
\text{Fe}^{2+} + 6\text{CN}_2^- \rightleftharpoons \text{FeCN}_6^{4-}
\]
The initial concentrations are:
\[
[ \text{Fe}^{2+} ] = 1.0 \text{ M} \\
[ \text{CN}_{2-} ] = 0.25 \text{ M} \\
[ \text{FeCN}_6^{4-} ] = 0 \text{ M}
\]
When 0.25 moles of KCN are added to 1 L of 1.0 M FeCl$_3}$ solution, the concentration of C\(^-\) increases by 0.25 M. However, the reaction involves 6 C\(^-\) for each Fe\(2+\). Thus, the change in concentration of Fe\(2+\) is:
\[
\Delta[\text{Fe}^{2+}] = - \frac{1}{6} \Delta[\text{CN}_{2-}]}
\]
The change in concentration of C\(2-\) is 0.25 M, so the change in concentration of Fe\(2+\) is:
\[
\Delta[\text{Fe}^{2+]} = - \frac{1}{6} ( 0.25 ) = - 0.022 \text{ M}
\]
The change in concentration of Fe\(2+) is - 0.022 M, so the change in concentration of Fe\(2+\) is:
\[
\Delta[\text{FeCN}_6^{4-}] = + 0.022 \text{ M}
\]
The equilibrium concentrations are:
\[
[\text{Fe}^{2+}] = 1.0 - 0.022 = 0.978 \text{ M} \\
[\text{CN}_{2-}] = 0.25 - 6( 0.022 ) = 0.25 - 0.132 = 0.118 \text{ M} \\
[\text{FeCN}_6^{4-}] = 0 + 0.022 = 0.022 \text{ M}
\]
The equilibrium constant is:
\[
K_{eq} = \frac{[\text{FeCN}_6^{4-]}}{ [\text{Fe}^{2+}][ [\text{CN}_{2-}]^6 ]} = 1.2 \times 10^{20}
\]
Using the equilibrium concentrations:
\[
1.2 \times 10^{20} = \frac{0.022 }{ 0.978 \times ( 0.118 ) } = \frac{0.022 }{ 1.171 \text{ M}} = 18.
