Calculate the entropy change (J/mol K) of the given reaction. The molar entropies [J/K mol] are given in brackets after each substance.

\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} [91.2] + 3 \text{O}_2(g) [205.1] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} [66.5] + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) [248.2] \]
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The entropy change (\(\Delta G\)) for a reaction is calculated using the formula:
\[
\Delta G = \sumn_{\text{products}} \epsilon_{\text{product}} - \sumn_{\text{products}} \varepsilon_{\text{product}}
\]
where \(\varepsilon_{\text{reactants}}\) and \(\varepsilon_{\text{products}}\) are the molar entropities of the products and reactants, respectively.

For the given reaction:
\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g] \]
The molar entropities are:
\[
\text{PbS}(s) = 91.2 \, \text{J/K mol} \\
\text{O}_2(g) = 205.1 \, \text{J/K mol} \\
\text{PbO}(s) = 66.5 \, \text{J/K mol} \\
\text{SO}_2}(g) = 248.2 \, \text{J/K mol}
\]
Substituting the values into the formula:
\[
\Delta G = ( 2 \times 66.5 + 2 \times 248.2 ) - ( 2 \times 91.2 + 3 \times 205.1 ) \]
\[
\Delta G = ( 133 + 504.4 ) - ( 192.4 + 616.3 ) \]
\[
\Delta G = 667.4 - 888.7 \]
\[
\Delta G = -221.3 \, \text{J/mol}
\]

Thus, the entropy change is \(\boxed{-221.3 \, \text{J/mol K}}\).
