","To solve this problem, we can use the principle of counting in the context of the normal distribution. The total number of houses is 90.
Number of houses with a two-car garage = 50.
Number of houses with an in-the-ground swimming pool = 40.
Number of houses with neither a two-car garage nor an in-the-ground swimming pool = 35.

The number of houses with either a two-car garage or an in-the-ground swimming pool is the total number of houses minus the number of houses with neither a two-car garage nor an in-the-ground swimming pool.
Number of houses with either a two-car garage or an in-the-ground swimming pool = 90 - 35 = 55.

The number of houses with either a two-car garage or an in-the-ground swimming pool is given by the sum of the number of houses with a two-car garage and the number of houses with an in-the-ground swimming pool, minus the number of houses with both a two-car garage and an in-the-ground swimming pool.
Number of houses with either a two-car garage or an in-the-ground swimming pool = (Number of houses with a two-car garage) + (Number of houses with an in-the-ground swimming pool) - (Number of houses with both a two-car garage and an in-the-ground swimming pool).
55 = 50 + 40 - (Number of houses with both a two-car garage and an in-the-ground swimming pool).
55 = 90 - (Number of houses with both a two-car garage and an in-ground swimming pool).
Number of houses with both a two-car garage and an in-the-ground swimming pool = 90 - 55 = 35.

Thus, there are 35 houses that have both a two-car garage and an in-the-ground swimming pool.

