","To calculate the magnetic field strength at the center of a circular coil, we use the formula for the magnetic field at the center of a circular coil:

\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 N Q}{2 R}
\]

where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength in Tesla (T).
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of air, which is \( 10^{-6} \, \text{T}\cdot \text{m/A} \).
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil.
- \( Q \) is the current in the coil in Amperes (A).
- \( R \) is the radius of the coil in meters (m).

In this case, we are given:
- \( N = 100 \) turns.
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \).
- \( r = 0.2 \, \text{m} \).

Substituting these values into the formula:

\[
B = \frac{( 10^{-6} \, \text{T}\cdot \text{m/A}) \times 100 \, \text{turns} \times 1 \, \text{A}}{ 2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = \frac{100 \times 10^{-6}}{ 0.4} \, \text{T} = 250 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T} = 2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \( 2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T} \).

\[
B = 2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T} = 0.25 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{T} = 0.25 \mu\text{T}
\]

Final Answer:
\[
\boxed{2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}}
\]",fluency,"# Task Description
