## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
You’re pulling a chest of drawers, applying a force of 60.0 N at an angle of 60.0°. How much work do you do pulling it over 10.0 m?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the work done, we use the formula:  
\[
W = F \cdot d \times \sin(\theta)
\]  
where \( W \) is the work done, \( F \) is the force applied, \( d \) is the distance moved, and \( \theta \) is the angle between the force and the displacement.  
Given:  
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \).  
- \( \theta = 60.0^\circ \).  
- \( d = 10.0 \, \text{m} \).  
Substituting the values:  
\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times \sin(60.0^\circ)
\]  
\[
W = 600 \, \text{N m} \times 0.592
\]  
\[
W = 352.2 \, \text{J}
\]  
Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{352.2 \, \text{J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

