","To calculate the recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the principle of momentum conservation. The initial momentum of the system (the Earth and the cannonball) is zero, as both are at rest. After the cannonball is fired, the momentum of the system must still be zero. Therefore, the recoil velocity of the Earth must be equal in magnitude but opposite in direction to the velocity of the cannonball.

The principle of momentum conservation is:
\[ m_{\text{ball}} v_{\text{ball}} = m_{\text{earth}} v_{\text{earth}} \)
where:
- \( m_{\text{ball}} \) is the mass of the cannonball (1 kg).
- \( v_{\text{ball}} \) is the velocity of the cannonball (+90.0 m/s).
- \( m_{\text{earth}} \) is the mass of the Earth (5.97 \(\times 10^{24}\) kg).
- \( v_{\text{earth}} \) is the recoil velocity of the Earth.

Solving for \( v_{\text{earth}} \):
\[ v_{\text{earth}} = \frac{m_{\text{ball}} v_{\text{ball}}}{ m_{\text{earth}} } \]
Substituting the given values:
\[ v_{\text{earth}} = \frac{(1 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s})}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}} \]
\[ v_{\text{earth}} = \frac{90.0}{5.97 \times 10^{24}} \text{ m/s} \]
\[ v_{\text{earth}} \approx 1.52 \times 10^{-22} \text{ m/s} \]

